{
  "dom_id": "step-q154980",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/ae7d9bcbf9f3d4506b581eaebe7b1b8eaa7f3971b238747b8a9564b3b08df800.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?",
  "worked_solution": "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/7a8587793e8a82f04375b2976019c3f222a9e74a2d85784493a97cf36e5da5cc.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/5645587162a3292a764ee99b4b1126ec4e0b332586e38f67c634f1d4b44fca96.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14134102/assets/7fc369740f0487134c164b24a4c4fc47000045270e07a15ccdacacd6b389557c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
